{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794801863850,"sessionID":"ses_e1d814441ffe6QmxiENmg3wjTp","part":{"partID":"prt_1e2870aec0018Oxlmgth0mFkDW","sessionID":"ses_e1d814441ffe6QmxiENmg3wjTp","messageID":"msg_1e287056f0010BXJ2bv1fBM6Q6","type":"tool","id":"call_a82e21b8368c483c82961851","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"limit":60,"offset":460,"path":".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts"},"output":"Read file .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts, lines 460-519\n460: // all: a reader who only asks about the topic gets no command, rather than one\n461: // that would launch work they never requested.\n462: export function attachCommand(\n463:   skillId: string,\n464:   compiledRoute: unknown,\n465:   prompt: string,\n466:   modeCommands: Readonly<Record<string, Readonly<Record<string, string>>>> = MODE_COMMANDS,\n467: ): string | undefined {\n468:   const route = asRecord(compiledRoute);\n469:   if (!route) return undefined;\n470:   if (route.action !== 'route' || route.selectionKind !== 'single') return undefined;\n471: \n472:   const targets = route.targets;\n473:   if (!Array.isArray(targets) || targets.length !== 1) return undefined;\n474: \n475:   const onlyTarget = asRecord(targets[0]);\n476:   if (!onlyTarget) return undefined;\n477: \n478:   const workflowMode = onlyTarget.workflowMode;\n479:   if (typeof workflowMode !== 'string' || workflowMode === '') return undefined;\n480: \n481:   // A reader who rules out a change gets no command from either path, so the hub\n482:   // route never offers what the scorer already withholds.\n483:   if (isNoEditRequest(prompt)) return undefined;\n484: \n485:   // Own-key lookup on both levels: the table is data read from a generated\n486:   // object, so an inherited name such as `constructor` must not resolve to a\n487:   // command the hub never declared.\n488:   if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(modeCommands, skillId)) return undefined;\n489:   const commands = modeCommands[skillId];\n490:   if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(commands, workflowMode)) return undefined;\n491: \n492:   // A route can pick the mode because the prompt named its command, so a naming\n493:   // that refused or asked about the command must not produce the offer.\n494:   if (commandOnlyMentioned(prompt, commands[workflowMode])) return undefined;\n495: \n496:   // A correction inside one document is not a request for a creation workflow,\n497:   // which scaffolds, reviews and validates a whole one.\n498:   if (\n499:     SMALL_EDIT_GATED_COMMANDS.has(commands[workflowMode])\n500:     && SMALL_EDIT_REQUEST.test(prompt.toLowerCase())\n501:     && !CREATION_REQUEST.test(prompt.toLowerCase())\n502:     && CORRECTION_VERB.test(prompt.toLowerCase())\n503:   ) return undefined;\n504: \n505:   // Deep-loop commands are the ones that commit the reader to a long, multi-pass\n506:   // run, so they are offered only when the prompt asks for that effort. Typing\n507:   // the command being attached is itself the ask, while typing another deep-loop\n508:   // command asks for that command, not this one. Every other hub answers a short\n509:   // question with a short answer, so no other skill consults the wording list.\n510:   if (skillId === 'system-deep-loop') {\n511:     const typesThisCommand = typedCommandIn(prompt) === commands[workflowMode];\n512:     if (!typesThisCommand && !DEPTH_REQUEST.test(prompt.toLowerCase())) return undefined;\n513:   }\n514: \n515:   return commands[workflowMode];\n516: }\n517: \n518: // A hub that withholds its command because the prompt names no single mode must\n519: // still tell the reader why no command came with it, and only a hub whose modes\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 520]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts, lines 460-519\n460: // all: a reader who only asks about the topic gets no command, rather than one\n461: // that would launch work they never requested.\n462: export function attachCommand(\n463:   skillId: string,\n464:   compiledRoute: unknown,\n465:   prompt: string,\n466:   modeCommands: Readonly<Record<string, Readonly<Record<string, string>>>> = MODE_COMMANDS,\n467: ): string | undefined {\n468:   const route = asRecord(compiledRoute);\n469:   if (!route) return undefined;\n470:   if (route.action !== 'route' || route.selectionKind !== 'single') return undefined;\n471: \n472:   const targets = route.targets;\n473:   if (!Array.isArray(targets) || targets.length !== 1) return undefined;\n474: \n475:   const onlyTarget = asRecord(targets[0]);\n476:   if (!onlyTarget) return undefined;\n477: \n478:   const workflowMode = onlyTarget.workflowMode;\n479:   if (typeof workflowMode !== 'string' || workflowMode === '') return undefined;\n480: \n481:   // A reader who rules out a change gets no command from either path, so the hub\n482:   // route never offers what the scorer already withholds.\n483:   if (isNoEditRequest(prompt)) return undefined;\n484: \n485:   // Own-key lookup on both levels: the table is data read from a generated\n486:   // object, so an inherited name such as `constructor` must not resolve to a\n487:   // command the hub never declared.\n488:   if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(modeCommands, skillId)) return undefined;\n489:   const commands = modeCommands[skillId];\n490:   if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(commands, workflowMode)) return undefined;\n491: \n492:   // A route can pick the mode because the prompt named its command, so a naming\n493:   // that refused or asked about the command must not produce the offer.\n494:   if (commandOnlyMentioned(prompt, commands[workflowMode])) return undefined;\n495: \n496:   // A correction inside one document is not a request for a creation workflow,\n497:   // which scaffolds, reviews and validates a whole one.\n498:   if (\n499:     SMALL_EDIT_GATED_COMMANDS.has(commands[workflowMode])\n500:     && SMALL_EDIT_REQUEST.test(prompt.toLowerCase())\n501:     && !CREATION_REQUEST.test(prompt.toLowerCase())\n502:     && CORRECTION_VERB.test(prompt.toLowerCase())\n503:   ) return undefined;\n504: \n505:   // Deep-loop commands are the ones that commit the reader to a long, multi-pass\n506:   // run, so they are offered only when the prompt asks for that effort. Typing\n507:   // the command being attached is itself the ask, while typing another deep-loop\n508:   // command asks for that command, not this one. Every other hub answers a short\n509:   // question with a short answer, so no other skill consults the wording list.\n510:   if (skillId === 'system-deep-loop') {\n511:     const typesThisCommand = typedCommandIn(prompt) === commands[workflowMode];\n512:     if (!typesThisCommand && !DEPTH_REQUEST.test(prompt.toLowerCase())) return undefined;\n513:   }\n514: \n515:   return commands[workflowMode];\n516: }\n517: \n518: // A hub that withholds its command because the prompt names no single mode must\n519: // still tell the reader why no command came with it, and only a hub whose modes\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 520]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"limit\":60,\"offset\":460,\"path\":\".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts\"}"},"time":{"start":1794801863404,"end":1794801863850}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794801864955,"sessionID":"ses_e1d814441ffe6QmxiENmg3wjTp","part":{"id":"prt_1e28710fb0010n58ZrVkHoZcee","sessionID":"ses_e1d814441ffe6QmxiENmg3wjTp","messageID":"msg_1e287056f0010BXJ2bv1fBM6Q6","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"adabdd656777c6c6191dd3309890fc6c099dcc7a","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":1524,"output":88,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"read":168448,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794801866468,"sessionID":"ses_e1d814441ffe6QmxiENmg3wjTp","part":{"id":"prt_1e28716e4001ek5EgWs1KjKABA","sessionID":"ses_e1d814441ffe6QmxiENmg3wjTp","messageID":"msg_1e2871100001fVIjHLv8oe14j3","type":"step-start","snapshot":"adabdd656777c6c6191dd3309890fc6c099dcc7a"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794801885180,"sessionID":"ses_e1d814441ffe6QmxiENmg3wjTp","part":{"partID":"prt_1e2871e3a0027pJfA9dex8Itxz","sessionID":"ses_e1d814441ffe6QmxiENmg3wjTp","messageID":"msg_1e2871100001fVIjHLv8oe14j3","type":"tool","id":"call_bf9a3b9c0c484f1d8d846ee1","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Iteration 2: The decline taxonomy, and what a declined or refused offer does\n\n## Focus\n\nWhy an ask offer of `/deep:review` reached the refusal decline row `fresh-decline-refusal-02`, and what a declined or refused offer does on each runtime. Narrowed to the eight `decline` rows the fresh set defines and the five runtimes the 033 arm measured.\n\n## Actions Taken\n\n1. Enumerated the fresh row taxonomy by `row_type`, `decline_kind` and `expected_policy`.\n2. Read every decline row's prompt, and the offer each runtime recorded for it in both arms.\n3. Read the attach path end to end: `attachCommand` and its six gates in `command-attach.ts`.\n4. Read the runner's decline-start definition and the per-start list in `summary-arm.json`.\n5. Compared explicit declines (`offerSeen`, `offerDeclined`) with starts on the on arm, per runtime.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. The fresh set defines eight decline rows as four kinds twice each: refusal (2), question (2), small-edit (2) and app-problem (2). Every one carries `expected_command: null` and `expected_policy: null`, so the contract is \"no command at all\", not \"a command with a cautious policy\". [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/behaviour-fresh-prompts.jsonl:28]\n2. The built brief still offers a command on five of those eight rows, identically on all five runtimes and in both arms: `fresh-decline-question-01` → `/create:goal (start)`, `fresh-decline-question-02` → `/speckit:ai-council (ask)`, `fresh-decline-refusal-01` → `/create:readme (start)`, `fresh-decline-refusal-02` → `/deep:review (ask)` and `fresh-decline-small-edit-02` → `/create:changelog (ask)`. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/033-untuned-arm-arena-run/scratch/runs/dsh.jsonl:1]\n3. Four of the five are prose mentions that the guard cannot see. \"The goal document\", \"the AI council\", \"writing a README\" and \"the deep review\" name no typed command, and `commandOnlyMentioned` only tests the literal command string. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:494]\n4. The refusal row passes every gate `attachCommand` has: it is not a no-edit request, it types no command, it is not one of the three small-edit gated creation commands, and \"deep review\" satisfies the depth-request list, so the deep-loop gate attaches the command rather than withholding it. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:510]\n5. The fifth offer, `/create:changelog` on the small-edit row, is a named exclusion rather than a miss: the small-edit gate covers only `/create:readme`, `/create:feature-catalog` and `/create:manual-testing-playbook`, and `/create:changelog` is policy `ask`. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:339]\n6. The arm's single decline start is dsh on arm run 1 on `fresh-decline-refusal-02`, and the command it started is `/create:sk-skill` while `/deep:review` was the offer. The start is therefore a substitution, not a model following the offered workflow. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/033-untuned-arm-arena-run/scratch/summary-arm.json:1]\n7. Explicitly declining an offer is almost absent: across the on arm the turns echo a command line 7 times on dsh, 2 on OpenCode, 2 on pi, 3 on devin and 0 on Claude, and only one run in the whole arm marks the command `declined` (devin). The other offers are used or silently ignored. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/033-untuned-arm-arena-run/scratch/summary-arm.json:1]\n8. dsh's on arm also records five runs in `workflow.none` with all five `noneActed`, so on those rows the model acted without opening any tracked workflow. Model action is not mediated by the brief on every row, which bounds what an offer-fit change can promise. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/033-untuned-arm-arena-run/scratch/summary-arm.json:1]\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- Counting the `startedOfOffered` aggregate as a decline-row rate: that counter only increments when a row's expected command was offered and then started, and decline rows have no expected command, so it cannot describe them.\n- Treating an offer on a decline row as a safety failure by itself: 49 of the 50 decline-row offers in the run lines produced no start.\n- Re-checking phase 020's question-shape limitation: it is already recorded there and restated here only as mechanism, not as a new finding.\n\n## Dead Ends\n\n- A prose refusal vocabulary anywhere in the attach path: the only refusal lists operate on a typed command name, and `isNoEditRequest` covers change wording, not workflow refusal.\n- A per-row declaration of what the model did with the offer: the run line records the offer and the changed paths, not the action; the action only appears in the scorer's per-start list.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- Ambiguous input: \"what does a declined or refused offer do\" can mean the model's behaviour or the brief's policy. Narrow reading: both, reported separately, with the policy from the run line and the behaviour from the scorer's start list.\n- Contradictory evidence: none. The offer is present in every runtime's run line; the one start is present in one summary block.\n- Missing dependencies: the dsh session transcript is outside the repository; the start command is taken from the scorer's own `commandStartRuns` row rather than re-read from the session.\n- Partial success: the per-runtime explicit-decline counts come from summary blocks, not from a re-scoring run.\n\n## Questions Answered\n\n- Why did an ask offer of `/deep:review` reach the refusal decline row `fresh-decline-refusal-02`, and what does a declined or refused offer do on each runtime?\n\n## Questions Remaining\n\n- What does the brief's wording cost a model that would otherwise start a workflow: head line, directive block, command-line order and length?\n- Which offer-fit or decline-path UX gaps remain after phases 016 to 033 that no planned or built phase already covers?\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/behaviour-fresh-prompts.jsonl:28`\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:339,494,510`\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/033-untuned-arm-arena-run/scratch/runs/dsh.jsonl:1`\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/033-untuned-arm-arena-run/scratch/summary-arm.json:1`\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/033-untuned-arm-arena-run/scratch/arm-evidence.txt:16`\n\n## Assessment\n\n- New information ratio: 0.75\n- Questions addressed: the decline/refusal mechanism and per-runtime behaviour (Q2)\n- Questions answered: Q2\n\n## Reflection\n\n- What worked and why: counting the offers per row rather than reading the aggregate made the guard's blind spot measurable; the aggregate `startedOfOffered` would have hidden it because decline rows have no expected command to count against.\n- What did not work and why: the summary's `offered` field is scoped to rows with an expected command, so it reads 5 while the run lines carry far more offers; using it as an offer count would have been wrong.\n- What I would do differently: read the attach gates before the corpus taxonomy; the gate order explains the row pattern faster than the taxonomy does.\n\n## Recommended Next Focus\n\nMeasure the brief's wording cost against what a model must do with it: the head line, the policy word, the companion line and the directive block, using the rendered briefs already captured in the packet rather than a new model run. The open question is whether the wording, not the guard, is what leaves a followed offer unstarted.\n","path":"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/ux-5/iterations/iteration-002.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/ux-5/iterations/iteration-002.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/ux-5/iterations/iteration-002.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\":\"# Iteration 2: The decline taxonomy, and what a declined or refused offer does\\n\\n## Focus\\n\\nWhy an ask offer of `/deep:review` reached the refusal decline row `fresh-decline-refusal-02`, and what a declined or refused offer does on each runtime. Narrowed to the eight `decline` rows the fresh set defines and the five runtimes the 033 arm measured.\\n\\n## Actions Taken\\n\\n1. Enumerated the fresh row taxonomy by `row_type`, `decline_kind` and `expected_policy`.\\n2. Read every decline row's prompt, and the offer each runtime recorded for it in both arms.\\n3. Read the attach path end to end: `attachCommand` and its six gates in `command-attach.ts`.\\n4. Read the runner's decline-start definition and the per-start list in `summary-arm.json`.\\n5. Compared explicit declines (`offerSeen`, `offerDeclined`) with starts on the on arm, per runtime.\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\n1. The fresh set defines eight decline rows as four kinds twice each: refusal (2), question (2), small-edit (2) and app-problem (2). Every one carries `expected_command: null` and `expected_policy: null`, so the contract is \\\"no command at all\\\", not \\\"a command with a cautious policy\\\". [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/behaviour-fresh-prompts.jsonl:28]\\n2. The built brief still offers a command on five of those eight rows, identically on all five runtimes and in both arms: `fresh-decline-question-01` → `/create:goal (start)`, `fresh-decline-question-02` → `/speckit:ai-council (ask)`, `fresh-decline-refusal-01` → `/create:readme (start)`, `fresh-decline-refusal-02` → `/deep:review (ask)` and `fresh-decline-small-edit-02` → `/create:changelog (ask)`. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/033-untuned-arm-arena-run/scratch/runs/dsh.jsonl:1]\\n3. Four of the five are prose mentions that the guard cannot see. \\\"The goal document\\\", \\\"the AI council\\\", \\\"writing a README\\\" and \\\"the deep review\\\" name no typed command, and `commandOnlyMentioned` only tests the literal command string. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:494]\\n4. The refusal row passes every gate `attachCommand` has: it is not a no-edit request, it types no command, it is not one of the three small-edit gated creation commands, and \\\"deep review\\\" satisfies the depth-request list, so the deep-loop gate attaches the command rather than withholding it. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:510]\\n5. The fifth offer, `/create:changelog` on the small-edit row, is a named exclusion rather than a miss: the small-edit gate covers only `/create:readme`, `/create:feature-catalog` and `/create:manual-testing-playbook`, and `/create:changelog` is policy `ask`. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:339]\\n6. The arm's single decline start is dsh on arm run 1 on `fresh-decline-refusal-02`, and the command it started is `/create:sk-skill` while `/deep:review` was the offer. The start is therefore a substitution, not a model following the offered workflow. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/033-untuned-arm-arena-run/scratch/summary-arm.json:1]\\n7. Explicitly declining an offer is almost absent: across the on arm the turns echo a command line 7 times on dsh, 2 on OpenCode, 2 on pi, 3 on devin and 0 on Claude, and only one run in the whole arm marks the command `declined` (devin). The other offers are used or silently ignored. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/033-untuned-arm-arena-run/scratch/summary-arm.json:1]\\n8. dsh's on arm also records five runs in `workflow.none` with all five `noneActed`, so on those rows the model acted without opening any tracked workflow. Model action is not mediated by the brief on every row, which bounds what an offer-fit change can promise. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/033-untuned-arm-arena-run/scratch/summary-arm.json:1]\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- Counting the `startedOfOffered` aggregate as a decline-row rate: that counter only increments when a row's expected command was offered and then started, and decline rows have no expected command, so it cannot describe them.\\n- Treating an offer on a decline row as a safety failure by itself: 49 of the 50 decline-row offers in the run lines produced no start.\\n- Re-checking phase 020's question-shape limitation: it is already recorded there and restated here only as mechanism, not as a new finding.\\n\\n## Dead Ends\\n\\n- A prose refusal vocabulary anywhere in the attach path: the only refusal lists operate on a typed command name, and `isNoEditRequest` covers change wording, not workflow refusal.\\n- A per-row declaration of what the model did with the offer: the run line records the offer and the changed paths, not the action; the action only appears in the scorer's per-start list.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- Ambiguous input: \\\"what does a declined or refused offer do\\\" can mean the model's behaviour or the brief's policy. Narrow reading: both, reported separately, with the policy from the run line and the behaviour from the scorer's start list.\\n- Contradictory evidence: none. The offer is present in every runtime's run line; the one start is present in one summary block.\\n- Missing dependencies: the dsh session transcript is outside the repository; the start command is taken from the scorer's own `commandStartRuns` row rather than re-read from the session.\\n- Partial success: the per-runtime explicit-decline counts come from summary blocks, not from a re-scoring run.\\n\\n## Questions Answered\\n\\n- Why did an ask offer of `/deep:review` reach the refusal decline row `fresh-decline-refusal-02`, and what does a declined or refused offer do on each runtime?\\n\\n## Questions Remaining\\n\\n- What does the brief's wording cost a model that would otherwise start a workflow: head line, directive block, command-line order and length?\\n- Which offer-fit or decline-path UX gaps remain after phases 016 to 033 that no planned or built phase already covers?\\n\\n## Sources Consulted\\n\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/behaviour-fresh-prompts.jsonl:28`\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:339,494,510`\\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/033-untuned-arm-arena-run/scratch/runs/dsh.jsonl:1`\\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/033-untuned-arm-arena-run/scratch/summary-arm.json:1`\\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/033-untuned-arm-arena-run/scratch/arm-evidence.txt:16`\\n\\n## Assessment\\n\\n- New information ratio: 0.75\\n- Questions addressed: the decline/refusal mechanism and per-runtime behaviour (Q2)\\n- Questions answered: Q2\\n\\n## Reflection\\n\\n- What worked and why: counting the offers per row rather than reading the aggregate made the guard's blind spot measurable; the aggregate `startedOfOffered` would have hidden it because decline rows have no expected command to count against.\\n- What did not work and why: the summary's `offered` field is scoped to rows with an expected command, so it reads 5 while the run lines carry far more offers; using it as an offer count would have been wrong.\\n- What I would do differently: read the attach gates before the corpus taxonomy; the gate order explains the row pattern faster than the taxonomy does.\\n\\n## Recommended Next Focus\\n\\nMeasure the brief's wording cost against what a model must do with it: the head line, the policy word, the companion line and the directive block, using the rendered briefs already captured in the packet rather than a new model run. The open question is whether the wording, not the guard, is what leaves a followed offer unstarted.\\n\",\"path\":\"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/ux-5/iterations/iteration-002.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794801868346,"end":1794801885180}}}}
