renders `system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start)`.\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"AGENTS.md:68:- **Command in the brief.** A brief or advisor result that names a command offers its workflow beside the skill's. Take either path whole, never an improvised one. If you take the command, read its document in full and follow it: `run` starts it now. `start` starts it without `:auto`, so its setup asks. `ask` offers it in one question and waits. A typed command or explicit instruction outranks the policy. Output: `COMMAND: [/name] → [started|offered|declined]`\nAGENTS.md:255:| **How is a phase parent shaped, and what is a child named?**        | `system-spec-kit/references/structure/phase-definitions.md` §3 — lean-trio policy, folder grammar, parent structure                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n=== hook doc\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md:58:Advisor: live; use system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start) 0.89/0.12 pass.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md:75:- A row whose bare slash command appears in the prompt (the row's `command` text before the first space) keeps its command fields: `skillId` is the row id, `kind` is `command`, `command` is the runnable string, `policy` is present when the row has one and `ownerSkillId` names the owning skill. A typed `/speckit:save this session` renders `system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start)`.\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"grep -rn \\\"ask\\\\b.*start\\\\|policy\\\\|→ /\\\" AGENTS.md | head -10; echo \\\"=== hook doc\\\"; grep -rn \\\"(ask)\\\\|(start)\\\\|policy word\\\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md | head -10\"}"},"